{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J1", "U7", "U4"],
      "pins": ["J1.A4", "U7.2", "U7.1", "U4.3"],
      "nets": ["VBUS", "+5V"],
      "observed": "On the MCU sheet the USB-C receptacle J1 VBUS pin (A4) is wired to a global label named 'VBUS'. The same name 'VBUS' appears on the power sheet as the output of the power mux U7 (TPS2121RUXR, pin 1 OUT) and as the input of the buck converter U4 (AP63203WU, pin 3 IN). The mux's USB input pin 2 (IN2) instead carries the global label '+5V', which has no connection from the USB connector.",
      "expected": "Per the spec, the USB connector's VBUS pin must sit on the '+5V' net that feeds U7.IN2, and the internal pre-buck rail named 'VBUS' is downstream of the mux and must remain distinct from the USB 5V net.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch (sheet 1/4 & 2/4): J1 pin A4 -> 'VBUS'; power.kicad_sch (sheet 3/4): U7 pin 1 OUT -> 'VBUS', U7 pin 2 IN2 -> '+5V', U4 pin 3 IN -> 'VBUS'"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "J12 ... VBUS pins (A4/A9/B4/B9) -> +5V net -> U5 pin 2 (IN2) ... note this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux."}
      ],
      "impact": "USB 5V is tied directly to the mux output / buck input rather than to the mux's IN2 input. The power mux can no longer arbitrate the USB supply (IN2 floats); when the barrel jack is also present the mux drives VBUS to 12V while USB forces 5V onto the same node, causing back-feed/contention and defeating the seamless-switchover design.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U1", "U9", "U6", "U8"],
      "pins": ["U1.5", "U8.19", "U8.20", "U9.6", "U6.6"],
      "nets": ["NET0019", "NET0008", "NET0018", "NET0006"],
      "observed": "On the thermocouple sheet the shared SCK line of all three MAX31855 chips (pin 5) is net NET0019, and the three CS lines are NET0018/NET0008/NET0006. On the MCU sheet NET0019 lands on the GPIO04 pin, NET0018 on GPIO03, NET0008 on GPIO05, NET0006 on GPIO07 (and GPIO06/NET0009 is used for the WS2812 DIN).",
      "expected": "The spec (confirmed from firmware) maps SCK to GPIO5 and the three thermocouple CS lines to GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "thermocouples.kicad_sch: U1.5 SCK = NET0019; mcu.kicad_sch: NET0019 on GPIO04, NET0008 on GPIO05, NET0009 on GPIO06"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "SPI2 on GPIO5/GPIO10, CS pins GPIO4/6/7 ... sck on GPIO5 ... three independent CS Output pins on GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7"}
      ],
      "impact": "The SPI clock and chip-select signals are on different GPIOs than the firmware expects (SCK on GPIO4 instead of GPIO5, a CS on GPIO5 instead of GPIO6, and GPIO6 repurposed for the status LED). Sequential thermocouple polling would select the wrong device / clock the wrong line, so temperature reads would fail.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build deviates from the intended design: most clearly, the USB-C VBUS pin is wired to the internal 'VBUS' net (mux output / buck input) instead of the '+5V' mux-input net, bypassing the power mux; additionally the thermocouple SPI SCK/CS nets appear mapped to GPIOs that conflict with the firmware's GPIO5-SCK / GPIO4-6-7-CS assignment."
}

